"Doug Freyburger" <dfreybur@yahoo.com> writes:
> I'm interested in what the boundaries are, what is and
> isn't a fair ascension.

I think one boundary is that using outside-the-game means to find out
information about the specifics of your game in progress (rather than
about nethack in general) is "unfair."

For example, let's say you know a wooden ring costs $300. If you use
spoilers (or source-diving, or asking on Usenet, or wizmode wishing)
to find out what it might be, that's ok.

If you save your game, resume in wizmode, try on the ring, observe the
effect, make a note of what it is, quit (keeping the save file), then
resume your "real" game, I'd say you'd gone well beyond the bounds of
fair. You have an unearned positive ID of what wooden rings are _in
that specific game_.

Other specific things I think are cheating:

o- save-scumming

o- examining or altering the contents of the playground using means
   external to the game itself (e.g. with a hex editor)

o- bones-stuffing

o- using tools which automate non-trivial portions of the game play
   (as opposed to just making the interface nicer)

o- using a game built from sources which have been altered in ways which
   change the actual game play (as opposed to things like the dump patch)

Keeping written notes in player-space (to, e.g., mitigate the effects
of getting mind-flayered) is controversial. My personal opinion is that
it is okay, but I can see how reasonable people might feel otherwise.

As another poster upthread correctly pointed out, using Hearse means
you aren't playing solitaire anymore, and thus it actually matters (in
a way much more substantive than bragging rights) that you conduct
yourself in the most squeaky-clean manner possible.

On correctly-configured single-host multi-user systems most cheating
is impossible (except bones-stuffing, which is far less practical).


